From 486944d423278d79c6c844b784c6e538f3257c72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duck-workspace <duck-workspace@localhost>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 07:21:38 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Route torch to the CUDA index on Windows too

PyPI's torch wheel for win32 is CPU-only (2.9.1+cpu, torch.version.cuda is
None), so torch.cuda.device_count() == 0 and mjlab's select_gpus() indexes
an empty list -> IndexError before the first training step
(mjlab/utils/gpu.py:70). The same failure as on linux-aarch64, for a
different reason: Linux carries CUDA in the nvidia-*-cu12 dependencies,
Windows carries it inside the torch wheel itself, and PyPI's Windows wheel
is the CPU one. The upstream comment (the marker keeps x86_64 on PyPI,
where the wheel already bundles CUDA via its nvidia-*-cu12 deps) holds on
Linux and not on Windows.

Route torch AND torchvision to the pytorch cu128 index on win32. cu128 and
not cu130: warp 1.12.0 bundles CUDA 12.9, torch ships no cu129 Windows
wheel, and the zero-copy warp<->torch interop needs both on one CUDA
major. torchvision is a transitive dep of rsl-rl-lib and uv applies
[tool.uv.sources] to DIRECT deps only, so it is declared here too,
otherwise a CPU torchvision ends up next to a CUDA torch.

linux-x86_64 (HF Jobs) and macOS still resolve from PyPI, unchanged.

Also make the test suite pass on Windows:

  - read pyproject.toml / uv.lock / generated files with encoding utf-8;
    the default locale code page (cp936 here) cannot decode the UTF-8
    em dashes in them.
  - test_train_on_path_is_a_mjlab_trainer read the first 8 KiB of the
    train executable; the Windows console-script launcher embeds the
    target path at the END of the PE image, so read the whole file.
  - test_aarch64_cuda_torch asserted every torch source marker mentions
    aarch64 and every non-aarch64 entry resolves from PyPI. That proxy
    encoded the invariant x86_64 stays on PyPI, which is exactly the one
    that does not hold on Windows. Rewritten to assert the intent: aarch64
    keeps its CUDA route, every platform-scoped route names a PyTorch CUDA
    index, and the platforms that must stay on PyPI (linux-x86_64, macOS)
    still do. Added test_win32_resolution_uses_a_cuda_index.
  - test_infer_policy_bam loads scripts/infer_policy.py, which imports
    termios/tty at module level for POSIX raw-mode keyboard input; the
    module cannot be imported on Windows at all. Skip it there.
---
